## Suggested trigger for an epoll redesign

I would plan an epoll migration only when **at least one** of these becomes a concrete requirement, and especially when two or more apply:

| New requirement | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| More PSI triggers across memory, I/O, or per-cgroup PSI | **Use epoll.** PSI requires one FD per trigger, so this grows naturally. ([docs.kernel.org](https://docs.kernel.org/accounting/psi.html?from=20423&utm_source=openai)) |
| Multiple independent timers | Add timerfd(s) and migrate to epoll if timers coexist with PSI and other sources. ([man7.org](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/timerfd_create.2.html?utm_source=openai)) |
| Reload/debug/shutdown must be serialized with FD events | Use signalfd with epoll; block the relevant signals before creating the FD. ([man7.org](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/signalfd.2.html?utm_source=openai)) |
| Root-only runtime control API | Add a Unix socket only with an explicit protocol and credential checks; use epoll once accepting/serving clients is required. |
| Multiple zram devices | First make state/config/locks per-device; then epoll becomes reasonable because each device can contribute PSI/timer/device state. |
| Runtime device events or an internal async worker | Consider `eventfd` for internal wakeups; it is lighter than a pipe and can be monitored with poll/epoll. ([man7.org](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/eventfd.2.html?utm_source=openai)) |
| Dynamic FD registration/removal | **Use epoll.** This is a stronger signal than raw descriptor count. |

A practical threshold would be:

- **Stay with `IO::Poll`:** two PSI FDs, one timeout, ordinary signal handlers.
- **Start design work:** a third independent source with its own lifecycle or deadline.
- **Implement epoll:** five-ish active FDs, any dynamically changing watcher set, a multi-zram model, or a control socket with clients.

The number is not a hard performance limit. It is an engineering threshold: above it, centralizing registration, readiness, cleanup, and ordering becomes more valuable than preserving the simpler loop.

